{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/b1-explainer-about-finnish-history-from-the-first-mention-of-the-name-through-today-gather/audio/main-89d3b7965221.mp3","durationSec":318.024,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":9.28,"text":"One way to approach this history is to ask: what written records exist, where did the people come from, and how did names and languages change over time?"},{"start":10.04,"end":15.52,"text":"The first known written mention of the *Fenni* comes from the Roman writer Tacitus in 98 CE."},{"start":16.3,"end":23.22,"text":"However, his description of northern hunter-gatherers does not prove that they were the ancestors of modern Finns."},{"start":23.74,"end":28.04,"text":"Even the origins of the names *Finland* and *Suomi* remain uncertain."},{"start":28.92,"end":37.18,"text":"Archaeology shows continuous settlement in Finland since about 9000 BCE, but languages and people also moved over time."},{"start":37.98,"end":40.94,"text":"Ancestors of Finnish speakers came west in several waves."},{"start":40.94,"end":46.32,"text":"Finnish developed from Proto-Finnic, probably spoken south of the Gulf of Finland."},{"start":47.2,"end":55.7,"text":"It belongs to the Uralic family and is closely related to Estonian and Karelian—not to Indo-European languages such as Lithuanian."},{"start":55.7,"end":60.34,"text":"Sámi languages were once spoken across much more of Finland."},{"start":61.12,"end":65,"text":"From the late 13th century, most Finnish lands became part of Sweden."},{"start":65.64,"end":71.58,"text":"Turku was the main town, and Swedish was used in government, education, and elite life."},{"start":72.24,"end":78.22,"text":"In the 16th century, bishop Mikael Agricola created the first comprehensive Finnish writing system."},{"start":78.74,"end":84.44,"text":"His *Abckiria* primer appeared in 1543, and his Finnish New Testament in 1551."},{"start":85.14,"end":89.64,"text":"Swedish rule ended after the Finnish War of 1808–1809."},{"start":89.64,"end":92.94,"text":"Finland then became an autonomous Grand Duchy within the Russian Empire."},{"start":93.54,"end":98.94,"text":"It kept many Swedish laws, while Helsinki became the capital in 1812."},{"start":99.54,"end":111.32,"text":"Finnish national culture also grew: Elias Lönnrot published the *Kalevala* in 1835, and an 1863 language decree began raising Finnish toward official status."},{"start":112.32,"end":121.68,"text":"In 1906, Finland created a one-chamber parliament and became the first European country to give women both the vote and the right to stand for election."},{"start":122.9,"end":125.56,"text":"The Russian Revolution opened a path to independence."},{"start":126.64,"end":135.64,"text":"Finland’s Senate presented a declaration in December 1917, and Parliament accepted it on 6 December by 100 votes to 88."},{"start":136.88,"end":140.08,"text":"Soviet Russia recognised Finland soon afterward."},{"start":140.99,"end":145,"text":"Finland still celebrates 6 December as Independence Day."},{"start":145,"end":148.38,"text":"However, the new country quickly divided."},{"start":149.28,"end":155.14,"text":"From January to May 1918, the socialist Reds fought the government-supported Whites."},{"start":155.74,"end":157.58,"text":"The Whites won with help from German troops."},{"start":158.46,"end":164.78,"text":"The Battle of Tampere was the bloodiest battle, and prison camps caused many later deaths from hunger and disease."},{"start":165.66,"end":170.92,"text":"Altogether, about 38,000 people died in a population of roughly three million."},{"start":171.7,"end":174.24,"text":"The conflict left deep political and social wounds."},{"start":174.24,"end":182.34,"text":"Nevertheless, Finland adopted a republican constitution in 1919, and Kaarlo Juho Ståhlberg became its first president."},{"start":183.26,"end":188.5,"text":"The Soviet Union invaded Finland on 30 November 1939, beginning the Winter War."},{"start":189.26,"end":197.5,"text":"Although Finland had a much smaller army, Finnish forces stopped or defeated Soviet units in places such as Suomussalmi and Raate Road."},{"start":198.18,"end":199.8,"text":"The war ended in March 1940."},{"start":200.34,"end":208.24,"text":"Finland remained independent but gave up about nine percent of its land, and more than 422,000 Karelians were evacuated."},{"start":209.22,"end":219.53,"text":"Finland fought the Soviet Union again in the Continuation War from 1941 to 1944, this time alongside Germany as a co-belligerent."},{"start":219.53,"end":229.92,"text":"A major Soviet offensive captured Viipuri in June 1944, but Finnish forces stopped further advances at battles including Tali-Ihantala."},{"start":229.92,"end":235.9,"text":"The armistice required Finland to give up more territory and then remove German troops from Lapland."},{"start":236.76,"end":245.56,"text":"Across the two Soviet wars, Finland lost about 12 percent of its land, but it was not occupied and its democratic state survived."},{"start":246.56,"end":253.5,"text":"Modern Finland is a Nordic republic of about 5.6–5.7 million people."},{"start":253.5,"end":262.74,"text":"Finnish and Swedish are its national languages, while Northern, Inari, and Skolt Sámi have official status in parts of the Sámi homeland."},{"start":263.58,"end":266.04,"text":"This language map reflects the country’s layered history."},{"start":266.98,"end":279.44,"text":"Nature is central to Finland’s image: forests cover most of the land, and official counts include 187,888 lakes larger than 500 square metres."},{"start":279.44,"end":281.7,"text":"Sauna is another strong cultural symbol."},{"start":282.48,"end":290.1,"text":"Finland has about 3.2 million saunas, and Finnish sauna culture entered UNESCO’s intangible heritage list in 2020."},{"start":291.72,"end":296.54,"text":"Finland joined the European Union in 1995 and adopted euro cash in 2002."},{"start":297.4,"end":304.76,"text":"After Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Finland changed its security policy and applied to NATO."},{"start":305.2,"end":309,"text":"It became NATO’s 31st member on 4 April 2023."},{"start":309.9,"end":317.44,"text":"Modern Finland therefore combines local traditions, Nordic democracy, European cooperation, and new security choices."}],"words":[{"text":"One","start":0,"end":0.22},{"text":"way","start":0.22,"end":0.46},{"text":"to","start":0.46,"end":0.72},{"text":"approach","start":0.72,"end":1.06},{"text":"this","start":1.06,"end":1.28},{"text":"history","start":1.28,"end":1.7},{"text":"is","start":1.7,"end":1.92},{"text":"to","start":1.92,"end":2.1},{"text":"ask","start":2.1,"end":2.4},{"text":"what","start":3.06,"end":3.1},{"text":"written","start":3.1,"end":3.42},{"text":"records","start":3.42,"end":3.84},{"text":"exist","start":3.84,"end":4.22},{"text":"where","start":4.76,"end":5.06},{"text":"did","start":5.06,"end":5.22},{"text":"the","start":5.22,"end":5.36},{"text":"people","start":5.36,"end":5.58},{"text":"come","start":5.58,"end":5.88},{"text":"from","start":5.88,"end":6.2},{"text":"and","start":6.48,"end":6.74},{"text":"how","start":6.74,"end":7},{"text":"did","start":7,"end":7.18},{"text":"names","start":7.18,"end":7.46},{"text":"and","start":7.46,"end":7.66},{"text":"languages","start":7.66,"end":8.06},{"text":"change","start":8.06,"end":8.56},{"text":"over","start":8.56,"end":8.92},{"text":"time","start":8.92,"end":9.28},{"text":"The","start":10.04,"end":10.28},{"text":"first","start":10.28,"end":10.52},{"text":"known","start":10.52,"end":10.84},{"text":"written","start":10.84,"end":11.1},{"text":"mention","start":11.1,"end":11.44},{"text":"of","start":11.44,"end":11.64},{"text":"the","start":11.64,"end":11.8},{"text":"Fenni","start":11.8,"end":12.2},{"text":"comes","start":12.2,"end":12.72},{"text":"from","start":12.72,"end":12.98},{"text":"the","start":12.98,"end":13.12},{"text":"Roman","start":13.12,"end":13.38},{"text":"writer","start":13.38,"end":13.7},{"text":"Ta